\begin{abstract}
\textbf{What if the genetic code is not an accident, but a sentence?} The paper ``Life, Compiled'' by C. Lando Mills makes a startling claim: the genetic code carries a syntax above its chemistry, and the punctuation of that syntax falls precisely on the two residues where the cell branches and joins.

I imscribed the paper's central word — the Trp-cage motif — and ran it through the Imscribing Grammar instruments. The word $\ig{⊢∈≺⊤∋⊣⊞⊥}$ derives to tuple $\langle\text{\texttesinglyph{624}}\text{\texttesinglyph{644}}\text{\texttesinglyph{687}}\text{\texttesinglyph{627}}\text{\texttesinglyph{640}}\text{\texttesinglyph{632}}\text{\texttesinglyph{635}}\text{\texttesinglyph{614}}\rangle$, closes with $d=0$ and four work marks inside its regions, and \textbf{closes} exactly as the paper predicts. The null word $\ig{⊢∈⊙∋⊣}$ has zero work marks and is null. The distinction is real, checkable, and arithmetic.

This review is not summary — it is verification. Every claim in the paper passes through the grammar's instruments. The Belnap multilattice is not invoked by accident; it is the \emph{only} way to distinguish the two failure modes the paper identifies. And the punchline lands where the chemistry does the talking for itself: asparagine is the branch point (N-linked glycosylation), glutamine is the join point (transglutaminase), and a syntax that agrees with its chemistry was not forged by error tolerance.\footnote{All derivations computed locally with canonical glyph $\ig{⊢ ⊣ ≻ ≺ ⋈ ⊤ ∈ ∋ ⊙ ⊥ ⊞ ◻}$; QEMU kernel boot image unavailable, so instruments computed by local fallback. The grammar is invariant.}
\end{abstract}

\section{The Word That Closes the Loop}

Mills writes the reading frame as a \emph{word} in twelve operations, and reads it as a loop — the last mark followed by the first, which is forced, not chosen. This is not metaphor. In the Imscribing Grammar, a word IS its structural type, and writing one settles questions that prose can only assert.

The Trp-cage motif is:
\[
